Unfortunately, there is no built-in method in LINQ that can modify objects in-place like the hypothetical <code>SomeCommand()</code> method you mentioned. The reason is that LINQ is designed to provide an immutable view of the data, meaning that the original objects should not be modified. Instead, LINQ creates new objects that represent the modified view of the data.

To solve your problem, you can simply use a <code>foreach</code> loop after the serialization to set the additional property. For example:

<pre><code>var mappings = part
  .Descendants("DataMapping")
  .Select(element =&gt; serializer.Deserialize(element.CreateReader()) as Mapping);

foreach (var mapping in mappings) {
  mapping.Extra = "blopp";
}

return mappings;
</code></pre>

This ensures that the original <code>Mapping</code> objects are not modified during the LINQ query, but they are modified afterwards before being returned.

Alternatively, you could modify your <code>Mapping</code> class to include a default value for the <code>Extra</code> property, which would be set during the deserialization process. Then, you could skip the <code>foreach</code> loop and return the modified objects directly from the LINQ query:

<pre><code>public class Mapping {
  // ...
  public string Extra { get; set; } = "blopp";
}

// ...

return part
  .Descendants("DataMapping")
  .Select(element =&gt; serializer.Deserialize(element.CreateReader()) as Mapping);
</code></pre>

This way, the <code>Extra</code> property would be set to "blopp" automatically during deserialization, and you wouldn't need to modify it afterwards.

