One possible workaround for selecting a folder in JavaFX is to use a TextField and a Button to open a directory chooser dialog. Here's an example:

```java
import javafx.application.Application;
import javafx.scene.Scene;
import javafx.scene.control.Button;
import javafx.scene.control.TextField;
import javafx.scene.layout.VBox;
import javafx.stage.DirectoryChooser;
import javafx.stage.Stage;

import java.io.File;

public class FolderSelectionExample extends Application {

    private TextField folderField;

    @Override
    public void start(Stage primaryStage) throws Exception {
        folderField = new TextField();
        Button browseButton = new Button("Browse...");
        browseButton.setOnAction(event -> {
            DirectoryChooser directoryChooser = new DirectoryChooser();
            File selectedDirectory = directoryChooser.showDialog(primaryStage);
            if (selectedDirectory != null) {
                folderField.setText(selectedDirectory.getPath());
            }
        });
        VBox root = new VBox(folderField, browseButton);
        Scene scene = new Scene(root, 400, 400);
        primaryStage.setScene(scene);
        primaryStage.show();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        launch(args);
    }
}
```

This code creates a TextField and a Button in a VBox layout, and assigns an action for the button. When the button is clicked, a DirectoryChooser dialog is opened, and when the user selects a valid folder, its path is set to the TextField.

Note that this example requires JavaFX 2.2 or later, since earlier versions didn't have a DirectoryChooser class.

