One way to keep track of the changes in a document is to use MongoDB's built-in feature called Change Streams. Change Streams allow you to listen to changes in a collection in real-time and perform actions based on those changes.

To implement Change Streams in your Node.js and Express backend, you can use the MongoDB driver for Node.js. Here's an example of how to listen to changes in the "Patients" collection and log them to the console:

<pre><code>// Import MongoDB driver for Node.js
const { MongoClient } = require('mongodb');

// Initialize MongoDB connection
const client = new MongoClient(uri, options);
await client.connect();

// Listen to changes in the "Patients" collection
const collection = client.db('your-database').collection('Patients');
const changeStream = collection.watch();

changeStream.on('change', (change) => {
  console.log(change);
});</code></pre>

The `change` object contains information about the change in the document, including the previous and current values of the changed fields.

You can also use Change Streams to perform more complex actions based on the changes, such as storing the previous values in a separate collection or sending notifications to users.

