output:
One way to achieve random access to members of a struct in C is to use a union. A union is a special data type that allows you to store different data types in the same memory location. Here's an example of how you could use a union to access members of your struct:

<pre><code>struct node{
    int a;
    int b;
    char c;
    union {
        struct node* next;
        int offset;
    } u;
};

void getMember(struct node* tmp, char* member_name){
    if(strcmp(member_name, "a") == 0) {
        printf("%d\t", tmp->a);
    }
    else if(strcmp(member_name, "b") == 0) {
        printf("%d\t", tmp->b);
    }
    else if(strcmp(member_name, "c") == 0) {
        printf("%c\t", tmp->c);
    }
    else if(strcmp(member_name, "offset") == 0) {
        printf("%d\t", tmp->u.offset);
    }
    else {
        printf("Unknown member name.\n");
    }
}</code></pre>

In this example, we've added a union to the struct that can store either a pointer to the next node or an integer offset. The offset can be used to access members of the struct by adding it to the memory address of the struct. Inside the getMember function, we check the name of the member that the user wants to access and use a series of if statements to determine which member to print. If the user wants to access the offset member, we simply print its value from the union.

Note that this approach is not completely safe since it relies on the user to pass in a valid member name. It's also not very efficient since we have to check the name of the member every time the function is called. A better approach would be to use a library like libffi that allows you to dynamically access struct members in a safe and efficient manner.

