Yes, you can achieve this by adding a CSS class that applies the <code>transition-delay</code> property to the logo image, and then toggling this class on and off when the button is clicked. Here's an example:

HTML:

```
<div class="sidebar">
  <img src="logo-normal.png" alt="Logo" class="logo">
  <button class="toggle-button">Toggle Sidebar</button>
</div>
```

CSS:

```
.sidebar {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 300px; /* default width */
  height: 100%;
  background-color: #f1f1f1;
  transition: width 0.25s ease;
}

.sidebar.compact {
  width: 100px; /* compact width */
}

.logo {
  transition: src 0.25s ease;
}

.logo.delayed {
  transition-delay: 0.25s;
}
```

JavaScript (with jQuery):

```
$('.toggle-button').click(function() {
  $('.sidebar').toggleClass('compact');
  $('.logo').attr('src', function(i, src) {
    return (src === 'logo-normal.png') ? 'logo-compact.png' : 'logo-normal.png';
  });
  if ($('.sidebar').hasClass('compact')) {
    $('.logo').addClass('delayed');
  } else {
    $('.logo').removeClass('delayed');
  }
});
```

Explanation:

- We define the CSS classes for the sidebar, logo, and the logo with delayed transition.
- We use jQuery to attach a click event listener to the toggle button. When the button is clicked:
  - We toggle the sidebar's compact class.
  - We toggle the logo's source between the normal and compact images.
  - We add or remove the delayed class on the logo, depending on the sidebar's class.
- The CSS transition effect is applied when the logo's source is changed. If the delayed class is present, the transition-delay property is also applied, causing the transition to start after the sidebar has finished animating.

Note: This example assumes that the logo images are named "logo-normal.png" and "logo-compact.png" and are located in the same directory as the HTML file. You may need to adjust the image paths or file names to fit your situation.

