output:
The lock statement in C# locks on the object reference passed to it, not on the variable itself. So in the code snippet provided, the lock statement is actually locking on the reference to _Data, not on the DataModel instance itself.

Therefore, if you call StaticAccess.Data.a = 3;, only the retrieval of the _Data reference will be locked, not the assignment of the property value. This means that you would still need to implement locks on the individual properties if you want to synchronize access to them. 

In fact, the code snippet as written is not thread-safe because the lock statement is locking on a non-readonly static field, which could potentially lead to race conditions during initialization. It is better to use a separate lock object for synchronization in this case. For example:

<pre><code>public static class StaticAccess
{
    private static readonly DataModel _Data = new DataModel();
    private static readonly object _LockObj = new object();

    public static DataModel Data {
        get {
            lock(_LockObj) {
                return _Data;
            }
        }
    }    
}</code></pre>

Then, you can implement locks on individual properties of DataModel as needed.

